.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt VHANGUP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vhangup
.Nd hang up the controlling terminal
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn vhangup void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn vhangup
hangs up the caller's controlling terminal, the console or a
pseudo-terminal's slave, as if the line had dropped.
A program such as
.Xr login 1
or a getty calls it before it opens the terminal again for a new user, so that no
process of the old session keeps a working descriptor of it.
.Pp
The hangup does these things, in this order:
.Bl -bullet
.It
It posts
.Dv SIGHUP
and then
.Dv SIGCONT
to the leader of the session the terminal controls.
A caller that is that leader and does not want to end ignores
.Dv SIGHUP
first.
.It
It takes the terminal from that session: no process has it as its
controlling terminal any longer, and it has no foreground process group.
.It
It discards input not yet read and a pseudo-terminal slave's output not
yet read by its master.
In packet mode the master receives
.Dv TIOCPKT_FLUSHWRITE .
It gives the terminal the termios a new terminal has, as Linux resets a pseudo-terminal's and a virtual
console's:
.Dv ICRNL
and
.Dv IUTF8
input,
.Dv OPOST
and
.Dv ONLCR
output, 38400 baud with
.Dv CS8 ,
.Dv CREAD
and
.Dv HUPCL ,
.Dv ISIG
and
.Dv ICANON
with
.Dv ECHO ,
.Dv ECHOE ,
.Dv ECHOK
and
.Dv ECHOCTL ,
the usual control characters and
.Dv VMIN
1.
.It
It hangs up every open file description of the terminal, the caller's
own among them.
A read of a hung-up description returns 0, a write fails with
.Er EIO ,
an
.Xr ioctl 2
fails with
.Er EIO ,
or
.Er ENOTTY
for
.Dv TIOCSPGRP ,
though
.Dv FIONBIO
works, and
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLIN ,
.Dv POLLOUT ,
.Dv POLLERR
and
.Dv POLLHUP
at once.
A read that is waiting when the hangup comes returns 0.
A description shared by
.Xr dup 2
or
.Xr fork 2
is the same description, and is hung up too.
.El
.Pp
A description opened after the hangup works as any other.
A session leader with no controlling terminal that opens the terminal
again, without
.Dv O_NOCTTY ,
makes it its controlling terminal.
.Ss System call numbers
The call is 111 on i386 and 153 on x86-64.
The x86-64 kernel has it for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80
too, as
.Xr ia32 7
describes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std vhangup
A caller with no controlling terminal gets 0, and nothing is hung up.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/vhangprobe.c
opens a pseudo-terminal, makes its slave the controlling terminal of a
new session, turns its echo off, calls
.Fn vhangup
with
.Dv SIGHUP
ignored, and checks the old descriptor's read, write, ioctl and poll,
that the session has no controlling terminal, that a new open of the
slave works and has the default termios, and that a process of another
user gets
.Er EPERM .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller lacks
.Dv CAP_SYS_TTY_CONFIG
in the first user namespace.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr setsid 2 ,
.Xr console 4
.Sh STANDARDS
The call is Linux's, and its rules are those of Linux 6.12's
.Fn tty_vhangup_self .
